Medicare Part B premiums do not reduce your Supplemental Security Income (SSI) payments. Social Security deducts Medicare Part B premiums directly from Social Security retirement and disability benefits, but SSI is a separate program. Most people who receive SSI qualify for Medicaid, and state Medicaid programs typically pay their Medicare Part B premiums through a Medicare Savings Program.

The Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) released the final rates in a CMS fact sheet on November 17, 2026. The standard monthly Part B premium is $209.40 for 2027. That is an increase of $6.50 from the previous rate of $202.90. The annual deductible is $292, and you can review the full breakdown on Medicare.gov. You can also check your personalized notice in the my Social Security Message Center in December.